  "articlePanels": [
    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>Finding a van defect is not the problem. No record, or no proof you acted, is.</p><p>A defect in a folder with no closure does not protect you.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>A defect is not a failure. A defect with no record, or no proof you acted, is.</li><li>Each report needs who found it, when, what it was and what was done.</li><li>A report with no follow-up is the weak point.</li><li>A photo removes doubt about how bad it is.</li><li>Recording the decision to keep using a van matters as much as the defect.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The law",
      "heading": "Telling someone is not a system",
      "html": "<p>Under section 40A of the Road Traffic Act 1988, it is an offence to use, or to cause or permit another person to use, a motor vehicle or trailer on a road in a condition that creates a danger of injury. The section applies in Great Britain.</p><p>The DVSA guide says drivers' defect reports must be given to a responsible person, recorded, and kept with details of any repair. Phoning the depot manager leaves no proof the defect was known, checked or fixed.</p><p>Traffic Commissioners look at how defects are managed. Defects with no follow-up look like a failure of management, not a slip. The DVSA guide is written for commercial goods and passenger vehicles. A van fleet outside operator licensing is not its target, but the same record is good practice.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The fields",
      "heading": "Every report needs the same things",
      "html": "<p>There is no single set format. The DVSA guide asks for defects and repair work to be recorded. These are the fields most records hold.</p><p><strong>Registration.</strong> Which vehicle.</p><p><strong>Date and time.</strong> When it was found.</p><p><strong>Driver's name.</strong> Who found it.</p><p><strong>Description.</strong> Clear. \"Tyre issue\" is not enough. \"Near-side rear tyre: tread 2mm, small cut in sidewall\" is.</p><p><strong>Place on the vehicle.</strong> Which corner, panel or part.</p><p><strong>How bad.</strong> Is it safe to use? If not, it stops.</p><p><strong>Photo.</strong> Not required, but strongly advised.</p><p><strong>Action taken.</strong> Repaired, stood down or watched. Record it separately and link it back.</p><p><strong>Sign-off.</strong> Who said it could go back on the road, and when.</p><p>The photo is the part most often skipped. A photo of a cracked mirror before driving off is proof it was minor.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The template",
      "heading": "What goes on the form",
      "html": "<p>Use this on paper, or copy it into a digital system. Keep a pad in each vehicle.</p><p><strong>Van Defect Report. Driver completes:</strong> vehicle registration; date of check; time of check; driver's name; defect description; place on vehicle; photo taken (Yes / No); vehicle safe to use (Yes / No, and if No, do not move the vehicle); reported to; time reported; driver signature.</p><p><strong>Fleet manager or operator completes:</strong> action taken; repair done by; date back in service; manager sign-off.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Where paper fails",
      "heading": "Follow-up is the part that breaks",
      "html": "<p>The driver fills in the form. It goes to the fleet manager. It lands in a pile. The repair happens. The form is never updated.</p><p>Months later, DVSA asks. You hold a defect with no closure. It looks like you knew and did nothing.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Taking a van off the road",
      "heading": "When the van cannot be used",
      "html": "<p>Some defects are clear. A tyre with a bulge does not move. A cracked windscreen in the driver's sightline does not move. Faulty brakes are not a judgement call.</p><p>Others need a decision. A small oil seep is a defect, but may not stop the van.</p><p>Record any decision to keep using a defective vehicle: who made it, why, and the stop-gap. For example: \"booked for repair by end of week; driver told not to load beyond 500kg until fixed.\" A decision with no record looks the same as ignoring the defect.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Sources",
      "heading": "Official sources",
      "html": "<p>The DVSA guide to maintaining roadworthiness (updated 28 April 2025, applies in England, Scotland and Wales) says drivers' defect reports must be recorded, given to a responsible person, and kept with details of any rectification work for at least 15 months. In the guide, where it says must it is a direct legal requirement and where it says should it is best practice. Section 40A of the Road Traffic Act 1988 makes it an offence to use, or cause or permit another to use, a motor vehicle or trailer on a road in a condition that creates a danger of injury. The guide is written for commercial goods and passenger carrying vehicles. Official sources checked on 18 November 2026.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guide-to-maintaining-roadworthiness/guide-to-maintaining-roadworthiness-commercial-goods-and-passenger-carrying-vehicles\" rel=\"noopener\">Guide to maintaining roadworthiness (DVSA, gov.uk)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1988/52/section/40A\" rel=\"noopener\">Road Traffic Act 1988, section 40A: using a vehicle in a dangerous condition (legislation.gov.uk)</a></li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>Must I record van defects in the UK?</h3><p>For licensed goods vehicles, yes. The DVSA guide says drivers' defect reports must be recorded, with details of any repair. For other vans it is good practice, and it is your evidence if a defect is questioned. A defect reported only by phone gives no protection.</p><h3>Can a van be driven with a recorded defect?</h3><p>It depends. Some defects do not stop use, like a small cosmetic dent. Others mean the vehicle does not move: a tyre below the legal minimum, a cracked windscreen in the driver's sightline, a faulty brake light. The operator and driver share the call. Record the decision.</p><h3>What should a van defect report include?</h3><p>Registration, date and time found, the driver's name, a description, the place on the vehicle, a photo if you can, whether the van was taken off the road, and the date and type of repair. Keep the repair sign-off separate, but linked.</p><h3>How long must defect records be kept?</h3><p>The DVSA guide says drivers' defect reports and any record of repair must be kept for at least 15 months. Without an operator licence the guide does not apply, but 15 months is a sensible match. Digital records cost nothing to keep and are quick to search.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/van-check-app-uk.html\">Van check app for UK fleets</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/fleet-vehicle-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Fleet vehicle inspection checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/dvsa-walkaround-check-free-template-uk.html\">DVSA walkaround check template</a></li></ul>"
    }
  ],
